(project convention-abstract
 (doc "Convention as registered data, not a closed variant: one drift pipeline, one carrier adapter, one build/test declaration per language.")
 (status planned)
 (no-inline
 )
 (section why
  (doc "What the current Convention dispatch cannot survive")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Convention.t is today a two-constructor variant (Ocaml_dune |
        Go_standard) in lib/convention.ml, and language support is
        threaded through by hand at each site:

          - lib/check/drift.ml:716 dispatches run_code_drift to
            check_go_code_drift or check_code_drift — two functions,
            not one hook. Go got its own generate_go_findings and
            write_go_meta_files too (drift.ml:594, :694), so the
            "pipeline" is already three parallel copies deep.
          - ~41 hardcoded ".ml"/".mli" checks live outside
            convention.ml: surface.ml:225, code_quality.ml:194,
            drift.ml:99 and :242 and :447, generate/generate.ml:150,
            generate/agent.ml:250, metrics/stats.ml:162,
            review/semantic_review.ml:27/:56/:113, doc/doc_extract.ml:184,
            book/book_print.ml:63, inbox/inbox.ml:358.
          - bin/cmd/Check.ml runs a literal `dune build` in
            run_worktree, so --worktree fails on every non-OCaml repo.
          - bin/cmd/Plan.ml and bin/cmd/Make.ml gather their module
            list exclusively from Dune_parse, so the agent prompts
            hand an empty module list to the implementer outside
            ocaml-dune.
          - Convention.suggest_module_files takes _t and ignores it;
            it only resolves dune module lists.

        Adding a third convention in this shape means a third copy of
        drift, a third meta writer, and a third set of extension
        guesses. The rule for this chapter: if adding a language forces
        an edit to drift.ml, the abstraction is still wrong.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section convention-record
  (doc "One record, registered in a list, one module per convention")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Convention.t becomes a record of functions and data, and each
        language ships as a module that constructs it:

          type carrier = {
            line_prefix : string option;   (* "//" "#" — None = block *)
            open_delim : string;           (* "(*" *)
            close_delim : string;          (* "*)" *)
          }

          type unit_kind =
            | Package    (* dir is the unit: Go *)
            | Crate      (* build unit above modules: Rust *)
            | Distribution (* pyproject/setup: Python *)
            | Gem        (* gemspec: Ruby *)
            | Library    (* dune library: OCaml *)

          type t = {
            name : string;
            unit : unit_kind;
            component_ext : string list;         (* [".rs"], [".py"] *)
            interface_ext : string option;       (* Some ".mli" | None *)
            excluded_dirs : string list;
            conventional_files : string list;    (* Cargo.toml, ... *)
            index : string -> unit_spec list;    (* discover build units *)
            surface : string -> symbol list;     (* component -> exports *)
            component_paths : section_name:string -> string list;
            carrier : carrier;
            build : string list;                 (* ["cargo";"build";"--workspace"] *)
            test : string list;                  (* ["cargo";"test";"--workspace"] *)
            verify_methods : string list;
            naming : naming_rule;                (* Explicit | Zeitwerk | Snake *)
          }

        Registration is a list, not a match: Convention.all : t list,
        Convention.of_string resolves against it, and Convention.resolve
        reads the root (convention NAME) as it does today. The closed
        variant and its four match sites disappear at the same commit
        that migrates Go onto the record — Go is the free test case,
        since its behaviour is already known and already specced in
        docs/convention-go.borg.

        naming is its own field because section-to-path is not derivable
        from directory layout in every language (see convention-rust-cargo
        §unit-map and the Ruby note in §open-questions). Explicit means
        (implements path) wins; the convention default only applies when
        the spec is silent.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section unit-model
  (doc "Build unit and spec component are two different levels")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Borge's model today is one section -> one module -> one file.
        That holds for ocaml-dune (dune library over modules) and holds
        by accident for go-standard (the package dir IS the build unit
        AND the component), which is why Go needed only one level and
        why check_go_code_drift could be a single flat pass.

        Rust breaks the coincidence: `cargo build` and `cargo test`
        operate on crates, but a section like transcript-parse maps to
        src/transcript_parse.rs inside a crate. Python breaks it again:
        the dist is pyproject.toml, the component is a module or
        package inside it. Ruby: gem, then class file.

        So unit_spec carries both levels:

          type unit_spec = {
            unit_name : string;              (* crate/package/dist name *)
            unit_path : string;              (* dir containing its manifest *)
            manifest : string;               (* Cargo.toml, pyproject.toml *)
            components : component list;
          }
          type component = {
            comp_path : string;              (* relative file path *)
            comp_name : string;              (* module name for matching *)
            is_build_target : bool;          (* crate/lib/bin *)
          }

        Drift then reports at the level where the finding is meaningful:
        "unspecified-component" for a .rs file with no section, and
        "unspecified-unit" for a crate nothing admits to owning. Today's
        Go pass merges both into kind = "unspecified-package".
      |))
  )
 )
 (section carrier
  (doc "How annotations attach to bindings, per language")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Go works with zero new parsing code because the borge (* *)
        grammar nests intact inside Go's /* */ block comment delimiters,
        so Doc_detect and Borg_comment read it unchanged. That was luck,
        and it runs out immediately:

          go-standard   /* (* agent note (| ... |) *) */   unchanged
          rust-cargo    /* (* agent note (| ... |) *) */   unchanged, but alien
          python        # (* agent note (| ... |) *)       needs adapter
          ruby          # (* agent note (| ... |) *)       needs adapter

        A line_carrier adapter collects a contiguous run of prefixed
        lines, strips the prefix and the comment framing, and hands the
        reconstructed text to the EXISTING Doc_detect. One adapter buys
        Rust, Python, and Ruby; Go keeps its block form untouched.

        Contract the adapter must honour:
          - A blank line or a non-prefixed line ends the run.
          - Equally-prefixed runs merge; a run whose open-pipe is never
            closed is reported as Unterminated_carrier, not silently
            dropped.
          - extract_comment_content (doc/detect.ml:170) currently
            hardcodes a 2-char open and 4-char total; the offsets must
            become carrier-supplied, not string literals.
          - Adapter output is line-aligned to the original file so
            Doc_detect's binding_line matching still works.

        Splitting carrier out of the structural convention is the whole
        point: structure (dir layout, build unit, naming) and annotation
        syntax (block vs line) are independent axes, and Go only looked
        like one axis because Go is a block-comment language.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section discovery
  (doc "Ask the language's own tooling instead of parsing manifests by hand")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Dune_parse and Go_parse both hand-roll manifest parsing. For the
        next three conventions there is no reason:

          rust-cargo   cargo metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps
                       -> crates, targets, src paths, as JSON
          python       stdlib ast -> module-level def/class, __all__
          ruby         stdlib Ripper -> top-level class/module/def

        This is strictly better than Surface.ml's line heuristics
        (surface.ml:32): a real AST rather than prefix matching. Borge
        already shells out for git (drift.ml:130 read_git_head), so a
        subprocess is not a new dependency class — but it must degrade:
        if cargo metadata fails, drift reports Unit_discovery_failed
        rather than claiming zero findings.

        The index function is the single seam a new convention must
        implement. Manual manifest parsing stays available as a fallback
        for offline or locked-down environments.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section drift-pipeline
  (doc "One code-drift path that takes index, not one function per language")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Target shape of lib/check/drift.ml:

          let check_code_drift dir =
            let conv = Convention.resolve dir in
            let borg = File_utils.find_borg_files dir in
            let covered = build_covered_modules borg in
            let units = conv.index dir in
            unspecified_components conv covered units
            @ unspecified_units conv covered units
            @ check_orphan_files ~conv dir

        check_go_code_drift, generate_go_findings, and write_go_meta_files
        collapse into this path parameterised by conv. Meta generation
        takes conv.surface; there is no per-language meta writer.

        check_orphan_files needs convention input too: is_conventional_file
        (drift.ml:258) allowlists dune, dune-project, and *.opam, so every
        Cargo.toml and pyproject.toml reports as an orphan today.
        conv.conventional_files supplies that list.

        looks_like_path (drift.ml:99) enumerates extensions for verify
        apparatus existence — add .rs .py .rb .toml .gemspec .json, or
        check_verify_apparatus silently becomes a no-op on every new
        repo. Silent no-ops are the failure mode this chapter is written
        against.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section gates
  (doc "build and test come from the convention, not from a literal dune call")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Check.run_worktree (bin/cmd/Check.ml) is the CI gate and it
        hardcodes `dune build`. It becomes:

          run_step "build" conv.build
          run_step "test"  conv.test

        Same sourcing for Plan/Make module lists (drop the Dune_parse
        block, use conv.index) and for borge drift --fix's build check.

        Where a language has no meaningful compile step, the convention
        says so rather than pretending: python's build is
        `python -m compileall` for syntax only, and its real gate is
        `verify (types "pyright")` / `verify (lint "ruff")`. A convention
        may declare test = [] and then borge reports "no test gate
        declared" instead of passing silently.

        verify_methods stays a per-convention allowlist, but gains
        optional runnable forms so a verify item can be executed later
        without a schema change:

          (verify (test "test/auth.ml"))        ; existing form, existence-only
          (verify (test "cargo test"))          ; new: argv form, runnable
      |))
  )
 )
 (section migration-go
  (doc "Go is the acceptance test for Phase 0")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (build
   )
   (test
    "test/test_drift.ml"
   )
   (smoke
    "borge drift --dir ~/dev/cavitycalc"
   )
  )
  (details
   (doc (|
        Phase 0 is not done when the record type compiles; it is done
        when go-standard runs on it and nothing observable changes.

        Acceptance:
          1. Convention.all has exactly two entries (ocaml-dune,
             go-standard) constructed by the record, and grep finds no
             match on Convention.Go_standard outside convention modules.
          2. `borge drift` on ~/dev/cavitycalc, ~/dev/wyo.tech/gregor,
             and ~/dev/wyo.tech/teachings reports the same finding set
             as the pre-change binary. Capture both with --json and diff
             them; a difference is a bug in the migration, not a bonus.
          3. `borge drift` on borge itself (ocaml-dune) reports the same
             set. This is the regression that catches over-generalising
             the OCaml path.
          4. `borge check --worktree` on borge still builds.
          5. grep for ".ml" outside convention modules drops from ~41
             sites to the handful that are genuinely OCaml-specific
             (mli handling), with the rest reading conv fields.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section modules
  (doc "New and changed modules")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        New:
          lib/convention/convention.ml        — record type, registry, resolve
          lib/convention/carrier_line.ml      — line-comment adapter
          lib/convention/convention_ocaml.ml  — the existing OCaml rules as data
          lib/convention/convention_go.ml     — the existing Go rules as data

        Changed:
          lib/convention.ml     — deleted, superseded by the directory
          lib/check/drift.ml    — one pipeline, conv-parameterised
          bin/cmd/Check.ml      — build/test from conv
          bin/cmd/Plan.ml       — module list from conv.index
          bin/cmd/Make.ml       — module list from conv.index
          lib/doc/doc_detect.ml — carrier offsets supplied, not hardcoded
          lib/metrics/stats.ml, lib/review/*, lib/book/book_print.ml,
          lib/inbox/inbox.ml, lib/check/code_quality.ml
                                — read conv.component_ext instead of ".ml"

        Note on scope: the last group is why this chapter exists. Each
        of those files is a place where a third language would otherwise
        be silently wrong rather than loudly unsupported.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section open-questions
  (doc "Decisions this chapter deliberately leaves to the implementing change")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        1. Ruby section-to-path. Directory layout is NOT the namespace:
           Zeitwerk maps app/models/user.rb -> User, a gem maps
           lib/foo/bar.rb -> Foo::Bar, and Rails apps inflect. Ruby
           should require explicit (implements path) or a configured
           naming rule, and borge should refuse to guess rather than
           invent a mapping that looks authoritative.

        2. Whether carrier belongs in the record or is resolved through
           a separate registry keyed by extension. Separate registry is
           cleaner if two conventions ever share a language (e.g. a
           rails-app convention and a gem convention both want '#').

        3. Unit vs component reporting thresholds. A 200-crate workspace
           will drown in unspecified-unit findings; the answer is a
           (untracked path "reason") declaration, not a lower default.

        4. Whether `borge facts --json` (sections, status, implements,
           verify per file) ships with Phase 0. It is the thing that lets
           non-OCaml tooling consume spec state without linking borge,
           and it is cheap once the record exists.
      |))
  )
 )
)
